Where you buy your coffee matters as much as what you buy. A good roaster takes care of half the work for you: they pick good green coffee, roast it well, tell you about it, and get it to you fresh.

This is part four of [Buying & Storing Beans](/coffee/guides/buying-and-storing-coffee-beans/).

## What to look for in a roaster

- **A roast date on every bag.** This is the big one. If a roaster doesn't print it, they're not roasting with freshness in mind.
- **Details about the coffee.** Origin, region, variety and process at least, and the producer or farm when they can. There's more on what those mean in [Reading a Specialty Coffee Bag](/coffee/guides/reading-a-coffee-bag/).
- **A menu that changes.** Coffee is a crop with seasons. A roaster that rotates in new single origins through the year is buying fresh green coffee.
- **A range of roasts**, or at least a clear style. Some roasters lean light, some lean classic and chocolatey. Neither is wrong, but it helps to know which you're getting.
- **Whole beans**, and ground only if you ask for it.
- **Good answers to questions.** A good roaster is usually happy to tell you how they'd brew a coffee, or which one to try if you liked another.

## Buying local

A local roaster is often the easiest way to get very fresh coffee. You can usually buy beans roasted within the last few days, you can taste before you buy at their café, and you can ask them questions in person. You're also supporting a local business.

The trade-off is choice. One roaster has one style and a limited menu, so if you live in a small town, the options can be thin.

## Buying online

Buying online opens up nearly every roaster in the country, and even some overseas. It's a great way to try styles and origins you can't find nearby.

A few things help:

- **Check when they roast and ship.** Good online roasters roast to order or on set days each week, and ship within a day or two.
- **Watch the shipping.** Cost and speed both matter. A bag that sits in a hot truck for a week isn't doing you any favors.
- **Buy a bag or two at a time** rather than a big order you'll have to freeze.

## Coffee subscriptions

A subscription sends you coffee on a schedule, usually every one to four weeks. It's an easy way to always have fresh beans without thinking about it. There are three main kinds:

- **A single roaster's subscription.** You get that roaster's coffees, often a rotating pick of their menu, or the same coffee every time if you have a favorite.

  - **[Counter Culture](https://counterculturecoffee.com/)**

    A favorite for single-origin coffee, with a strong focus on sustainability and long-term relationships with farmers. Whole bean only, so you'll need a grinder.
  - **[Intelligentsia](https://www.intelligentsia.com/)**

    Simple tiers to choose from: blends and espresso, single origins, or "Intelligentsia's Choice," where they pick for you. A good all-around place to start.
  - **[Stumptown](https://www.stumptowncoffee.com/)**

    Their Roaster's Pick sends a different coffee each time, chosen by their roasters, with notes on where it was grown. Easy to love if you like a little surprise.
  - **[Blue Bottle](https://bluebottlecoffee.com/)**

    Clean, polished coffees with options for single origins, blends, espresso and decaf. A nice choice as a gift.
  - **[Onyx Coffee Lab](https://onyxcoffeelab.com/)**

    Bright, adventurous coffees, often from rare or experimental lots. Best if you already know you like lighter roasts and want to explore.

- **A multi-roaster subscription.** One service sends coffee from many different roasters, usually matched to what you say you like. It's a great way to explore, but check how fresh the coffee is when it ships.

  - **[Trade Coffee](https://www.drinktrade.com/)**

    A short quiz matches you with coffees from independent roasters across the US. You rate each bag, and those ratings shape what you get next. It's easy to pick a schedule, then swap, repeat or pause from your account.
  - **[MistoBox](https://mistobox.com/)**

    Instead of a matching algorithm, a real person picks your coffees from dozens of independent roasters. Your picks get better as you rate bags, and you can pause for as long as you need.
  - **[Crema](https://crema.co/)**

    You build your own queue (they call it a Brewlist) from a big list of roasters and coffees, and they ship the next one on your schedule. Good if you'd rather choose every bag yourself. Shipping in the US is free.

- **A frozen coffee subscription.** Not beans at all. The coffee is already brewed for you.

  - **[Cometeer](https://cometeer.com/)**

    Coffee from partner roasters is brewed, concentrated and flash-frozen into small capsules that ship on dry ice. Keep them in the freezer, then melt one in hot water (or pour it over milk and ice) for a cup in seconds. You don't need a grinder or brewer, but it costs more per cup.

What to look for:

- **Roasted to order**, or roasted shortly before shipping.
- **Easy to pause, skip or change,** for when you get behind or go on a trip.
- **A schedule that matches how much you drink.** Work out how many grams you use a week (a 16:1 [ratio](/coffee/guides/coffee-to-water-ratio/) is about 20 g a cup) and pick a bag size and schedule so you finish each bag in a few weeks.

## Grocery store coffee

It's not all bad, but it's harder to find good coffee there. Look for the same things: a roast date, real details about the origin, and whole beans. Some stores carry bags from local or regional roasters with recent roast dates, and those can be a great find.

## Finding what you like

The best way to find a roaster you love is to try a few. Buy a couple of bags from different places, take notes, and compare. Once you find a roaster whose style matches yours, sticking with them makes buying coffee a lot easier.

That's the end of Buying & Storing Beans. To go back to the beginning, head to the [overview](/coffee/guides/buying-and-storing-coffee-beans/).
